<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liu Chengyou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">931–951</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liu Chengyou was the second and last emperor of the Later Han in the Five Dynasties period. He succeeded his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-zhiyuan">Liu Zhiyuan</a> in 948 as a youth guided by senior ministers and generals. In 950 he had leading ministers killed and moved against the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-wei">Guo Wei</a>, who rebelled; Liu Chengyou was killed, and Guo Wei founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a> in 951.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Liu Chengyou was born on 28 March 931 at Yedu, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-zhiyuan">Liu Zhiyuan</a>, then a trusted officer of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang">Shi Jingtang</a>, and his wife Lady Li. He was given titles as his father rose: an overseer of the Hedong headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> in 941, and a general in 947, when Liu Zhiyuan proclaimed the Later Han. When his elder brother Liu Chengxùn died late in 947, the grief broke his father's health. Dying in 948, Liu Zhiyuan entrusted the young prince to Su Fengji, Yang Bin, Shi Hongzhao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-wei">Guo Wei</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The new government at once faced rebellion in the west. Zhao Siwan mutinied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, Li Shouzhen of Huguo Circuit proclaimed himself Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, and Wang Jingchong held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengxiang">Fengxiang</a>. Guo Wei, sent to command, won the army's loyalty with generous rewards and besieged Hezhong until, in the summer of 949, the outer city fell and Li Shouzhen killed himself with his family; Wang Jingchong also took his own life. Meanwhile Yang Bin became the chief decision-maker at court, and quarrels grew between the generals and the civil officials.</p><p>After the rebellions the emperor grew arrogant and frivolous and resented his ministers' control, which kept him from promoting his favourites and his mother's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhaozong-of-tang">Li Ye</a>. Yang Bin also opposed making his favourite, Consort Geng, empress. Against his mother's advice he plotted with Li Ye and others, and on 24 December 950 soldiers killed Yang Bin, Shi Hongzhao and Wang Zhang at the palace. He sent secret orders to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-wei">Guo Wei</a> at Yedu, and the families of Guo Wei and Wang Jun in the capital were slaughtered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Warned in time, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-wei">Guo Wei</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, gathering the armies that surrendered on the way. On 1 January 951 the imperial army, led by Murong Yanchao, fought him near the capital and dissolved as soldiers and generals went over. The next morning the emperor fled, was turned back at the Xuanhua Gate and was killed at Zhao Village. Accounts differ: the Zizhi Tongjian says soldiers killed him in the confusion, while the Old History of the Five Dynasties says Guo Yunming killed him. Guo Wei, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a>, had him buried as an emperor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liu Chengyou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He had marched on the capital after the young emperor <b>Liu Chengyou</b> had his family killed in Kaifeng.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-11">11 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Liu Zhiyuan died in 948, leaving his young son <b>Liu Chengyou</b> under a group of senior ministers and generals.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-han">Later Han</a></li><li><blockquote>Guo Wei, a leading general of the Later Han, was forced into rebellion when Emperor <b>Liu Chengyou</b> plotted to kill him in 950.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ye, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.28,114.4&amp;year=944" aria-label="Ye on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7429 855 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Liu Chengyou"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7879 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7879 1125h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.28,114.4&amp;year=944">What was here in 944 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
